WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.360
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.780
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes

7
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.400
stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

8
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office ladies.

9
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:23.600
Hello.

10
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:24.620
Hi.

11
00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.380
We're really tired today.

12
00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:28.720
We are.

13
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.580
You guys, we still have young kids.

14
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.020
I had one that just could not fall asleep last night.

15
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.740
I just had insomnia last night.

16
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:36.840
Oh, that's rough.

17
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.320
So we're two very unrested people.

18
00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:45.160
And yet, I'm very excited about today's episode, The Delivery Part 1.

19
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:46.460
This is huge.

20
00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:47.880
That's what she said.

21
00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:53.320
Before we get into this, can I just say congratulations to Sam, Cassie, Jenna, and myself?

22
00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:54.940
Because we won a Webby.

23
00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:55.800
We did.

24
00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:56.600
Yes.

25
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:57.120
Yes.

26
00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:58.520
Congratulations, you guys.

27
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.780
And thank you so much for voting for us.

28
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:01.720
We love you guys.

29
00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:02.640
We love doing this.

30
00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.180
And we just wanted to start off with a thank you.

31
00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:07.120
Well, I think that's a great way to start.

32
00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.920
Angela has also started her day, even though you're tired, wearing your Bassmaster merch.

33
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.860
You're all dressed to exercise.

34
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:20.860
And I'm so impressed because, I mean, I don't exercise on days when I don't sleep well.

35
00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:25.080
I thought if I wore, like, sporty stuff, there might be a chance I'd be sporty.

36
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.820
And I love rocking my Bassmaster's hoodie, guys.

37
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:30.700
It's really cute.

38
00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:31.140
Mm-hmm.

39
00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:33.880
Bassmaster is doing some good merch.

40
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.640
Shout out to the Bassmaster merch.

41
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:37.220
Shout them out.

42
00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:38.220
All right.

43
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:45.760
Today is Season 6, Episode 17, written by Daniel Chun and directed by Seth Gordon.

44
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:47.960
I'm going to give you a summary.

45
00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:49.360
Do it.

46
00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.540
Pam has gone into labor at work.

47
00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:53.500
I'm sorry.

48
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:54.360
I need a lamp.

49
00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.580
I'm sorry to interrupt you.

50
00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:59.220
I don't have a lamp, and I can't really see, so I'm going to drag one over one second.

51
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.120
Okay, no problem.

52
00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:01.880
Tired eyes need a lamp.

53
00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.080
You know what?

54
00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.080
But I stumbled on the summary, so I'm going to give it another shot.

55
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.380
Maybe give it another shot.

56
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:11.200
Now I can see.

57
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.040
Well, now I'm jealous of all your light.

58
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.320
You want me to drag a lamp over for you?

59
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:16.580
Jenna, you can have a lamp.

60
00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:17.940
You know what?

61
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.220
Everybody gets a lamp.

62
00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:20.520
Cassie, you want a lamp?

63
00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.020
I can only hear that in the Oprah voice.

64
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.120
She's like, you get a lamp.

65
00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:24.920
You get a lamp, and you get a lamp.

66
00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:26.800
All right.

67
00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:30.600
I just want to say, you made fun of me when we were writing our book.

68
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.800
You made fun of me when I was cleaning out my garage,

69
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.080
and I had all those old lamps saved.

70
00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:36.620
Who wants a lamp now?

71
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:38.860
Who wants a working lamp?

72
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.020
With a little rewiring.

73
00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.020
All those lamps are good lamps.

74
00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:43.740
Here's my question.

75
00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.480
You ask me when I'm going to use all the baskets I hoard,

76
00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.420
but when are you going to rewire a lamp, lady?

77
00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:50.200
When's that happening?

78
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.040
I think you should know that in my retirement,

79
00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.960
I am going to become a lamp rewiring expert.

80
00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.100
Oh, I can't wait for our garage sales when we're old ladies

81
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.080
with our rewired lamps and our baskets.

82
00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:06.980
All right.

83
00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:09.420
Let's take another stab at the summary, shall we?

84
00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:10.140
Let's do it.

85
00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:10.600
All right.

86
00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.780
Pam has gone into labor at work,

87
00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:17.680
and she doesn't want to check into the hospital until after midnight

88
00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:21.420
because their health insurance only covers a two-night stay.

89
00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.960
Meanwhile, Dwight hatches a plan to have his own baby

90
00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.840
so he can be a more competitive salesperson.

91
00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:31.640
That's what's happening.

92
00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:32.980
It's a lot happening.

93
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:34.940
Fast fact number one,

94
00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.060
this episode originally aired on March 4th, 2010,

95
00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:43.460
and you know this episode is two parts.

96
00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:45.640
There's a part one and a part two.

97
00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.920
They were both aired together as a one-hour special.

98
00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.740
It was later separated into two episodes,

99
00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.140
which was done for syndication

100
00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.200
because syndication only takes half-hour episodes.

101
00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.260
Each of these parts were actually written and directed

102
00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:03.540
by completely different teaMs.

103
00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:05.760
So part one was written by Danny Chun

104
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.080
and directed by Seth Gordon.

105
00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.460
That's what we're talking about today.

106
00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:11.320
And part two was written by Charlie Grandy

107
00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:12.900
and directed by Harold Ramis.

108
00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:15.260
And while they were separate episodes,

109
00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:18.780
they did all kind of consult on the whole thing.

110
00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.780
Yeah, you know, there is a DVD commentary for this episode

111
00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:24.300
and on it are you, Greg Daniels,

112
00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.440
the writers of part one and two,

113
00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.040
Danny Chun and Charlie Grandy,

114
00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.940
and the director of the first delivery, Seth Gordon.

115
00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:36.120
And Jenna, right out of the gate on the DVD commentary,

116
00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:40.660
the very first thing said was Greg asking you

117
00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.600
what it was like to wear the fake pregnancy belly

118
00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:45.420
and if you were happy to be rid of it.

119
00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.400
Oh, what did I say?

120
00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:49.680
You had a lot to say.

121
00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.860
A lot that you've shared here about how uncomfortable it was

122
00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.500
and how long it took you to go pee and change clothes.

123
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.540
But it just cracked me up that the first thing talked about

124
00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:00.520
on the delivery was your fake belly.

125
00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:02.520
Well, in this episode,

126
00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.720
I have on the largest of all the bellies

127
00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.580
because this is her birthing day.

128
00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:10.100
And it was the most uncomfortable.

129
00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:11.960
But I have to say,

130
00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:14.140
I don't know if I mentioned this on the commentary,

131
00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:18.500
I continued to wear a belly for several episodes after this

132
00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:19.880
because in real life,

133
00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.240
a woman's belly doesn't just like shrink back to normal

134
00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:24.580
after they have a baby.

135
00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.520
So after Pam has her baby,

136
00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:30.640
I continued to wear like belly B for a while.

137
00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:32.980
You know, Angela Martin did not.

138
00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:33.760
I know.

139
00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:34.700
We'll get there.

140
00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:35.300
We'll get there.

141
00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:37.780
There's also some great deleted scenes

142
00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:38.780
that didn't make it in

143
00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:40.500
and some scenes that are in the shooting draft.

144
00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.360
I'm going to sprinkle those throughout the episode.

145
00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:43.140
Oh, great.

146
00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:47.260
Fast fact number two is a fan question

147
00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.620
from Dana in Clarksville, Tennessee.

148
00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:52.420
Why on earth does Pam choose

149
00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.240
to have all her major life events in the office?

150
00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:57.800
She came to work before her wedding.

151
00:05:58.060 --> 00:05:59.660
She came to work before art school.

152
00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:02.980
And now she is choosing to fully labor at work

153
00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:05.140
instead of in the comfort of her own home.

154
00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:09.180
Is the Dunder Mifflin paid time off policy that bad?

155
00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:11.040
This is a hilarious question.

156
00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:11.860
I love it.

157
00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:12.840
Yeah.

158
00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:15.240
I love that she's like, Pam, what is up?

159
00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.460
Dana, I hear you.

160
00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.000
We talked about why did Holly come to work

161
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:21.560
before she moved to Nashua?

162
00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:22.400
I know.

163
00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:25.860
She came to work for 30 minutes in her work clothes

164
00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.740
and then got into a giant truck and drove to Nashua.

165
00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:32.900
Well, writer Danny Chun addresses this question

166
00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:36.020
in an interview that he did for OfficeTelly.com

167
00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:37.820
shortly after this episode aired.

168
00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:41.940
OfficeTelly.com has great interviews

169
00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:43.640
with cast and crew members,

170
00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.920
like immediately with cast and crew members

171
00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:47.780
that are just terrific.

172
00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:48.980
Y'all should check them out.

173
00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:50.980
Danny said that the biggest challenges

174
00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:52.960
in making this episode were,

175
00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.340
number one, making a baby episode

176
00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:58.320
that didn't feel like a retread

177
00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.560
of every other sitcom baby episode.

178
00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:04.380
And number two, making a baby episode

179
00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:06.440
for a workplace comedy,

180
00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:08.560
because that's what we are.

181
00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:09.980
Oh, that's so true.

182
00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:11.180
Yeah.

183
00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:14.740
He said, ultimately, problem number two

184
00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:16.560
helped with problem number one,

185
00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:19.160
because one thing you haven't seen a lot of

186
00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:21.520
is people laboring in their workplace.

187
00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:22.260
It's true.

188
00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:22.940
So.

189
00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:25.220
Yeah, you know, I'm sitting here

190
00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:27.320
trying to think of shows that we watched growing up

191
00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:28.900
and did anyone go into labor

192
00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:31.840
and spend their day of labor at work?

193
00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:33.020
And I can't think of one.

194
00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:34.520
So this does feel really unique.

195
00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:36.400
I think they figured it out.

196
00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:36.880
Yeah.

197
00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:39.080
Well, fastback number three

198
00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:41.060
is a question from Evelyn in Michigan

199
00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:42.600
who wanted to know,

200
00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:44.980
Jenna, had you ever been pregnant

201
00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:46.720
before this episode filmed?

202
00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:49.980
And if not, how did you prepare for the part?

203
00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.600
She also wants to know,

204
00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:54.380
do I ever look back at the scenes

205
00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:55.840
now that I do have kids

206
00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:59.080
and think about maybe parts of my performance

207
00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:00.760
that I would have added or taken away

208
00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:02.740
now that I know what real labor is like?

209
00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.260
And do I think the acting would have been easier

210
00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:06.660
if I had been through it?

211
00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:07.800
All right.

212
00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:09.120
I'm going to unpack this, Evelyn.

213
00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:10.340
So first of all, no,

214
00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:12.600
I did not have kids in real life.

215
00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:13.920
I had not been pregnant before

216
00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:15.780
when I filmed this episode.

217
00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:17.660
So after I got the script

218
00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:20.460
and I realized I would be doing a lot of laboring,

219
00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.260
I wanted to know what does that look like?

220
00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:24.880
So I watched a ton of YouTube videos.

221
00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:27.560
This is just, I have to say,

222
00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:28.600
an actor's dream.

223
00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:31.920
So actual videos of people like going into labor,

224
00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:33.400
like maybe their spouse filmed them

225
00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:34.600
or something on YouTube?

226
00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:37.520
Yeah, and they have like baby stories of,

227
00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:41.360
you know, documentaries of the labor process

228
00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.120
from contraction to having a baby.

229
00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:44.880
Wow.

230
00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:46.520
So I just watched a lot of stuff

231
00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:49.300
about what happens to you when you're in labor

232
00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:51.060
and what it really looks like.

233
00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:52.780
You know?

234
00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:53.940
That's really cool.

235
00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:54.360
Yeah.

236
00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:56.680
But I have to say,

237
00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:00.620
I was really confused by Pam's choice

238
00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:02.220
to not go to the hospital.

239
00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:06.020
I understood the logic behind why she wasn't going,

240
00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:09.500
but I did go to the writers and say,

241
00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:12.580
I just am not understanding

242
00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:16.560
why a woman would put off going to the hospital

243
00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:17.540
for so long.

244
00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:19.520
Especially with her first pregnancy.

245
00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:20.140
Yes.

246
00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.840
And one of the writers told me

247
00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:26.100
that their friend actually did this,

248
00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:28.880
that she put off going to the hospital.

249
00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:31.420
It was not because of like an HMO issue,

250
00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.600
but because she was afraid to give birth.

251
00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:37.560
So she was like in denial.

252
00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:39.280
Her family was like,

253
00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:41.140
you are going to give birth.

254
00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:42.280
Like it is happening.

255
00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:43.100
And she was like,

256
00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:44.320
no, I think I'm good.

257
00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:45.580
I think I'm fine.

258
00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:47.520
And they had to like really

259
00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:51.380
like talk her into the reality of her situation.

260
00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:54.860
Ultimately, we find out that's why Pam's not going.

261
00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:55.840
You know, we'll get there.

262
00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:58.880
But that gave me something to hold on to

263
00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:01.220
that this wasn't just in Pam's mind.

264
00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:03.860
This wasn't just about an HMO thing.

265
00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:06.280
This was a little bit of like denial.

266
00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:07.080
Right.

267
00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:09.560
And she was sort of cloaking it with the HMO.

268
00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:10.160
Yes.

269
00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:12.140
Well, Greg shared on the commentary

270
00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:15.320
that he was a big champion of this HMO storyline.

271
00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:18.700
